WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.954
This is Mars.

00:00:00.954 --> 00:00:08.367
Every marker on the surface is the resting place of a spacecraft humans built on Earth and sent here.

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:10.276
Mariner 4 flew by in 1965.

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:14.606
It took the first close-up photographs of any other planet.

00:00:14.606 --> 00:00:17.983
Mars 2, a Soviet lander, crashed here in 1971.

00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:23.634
Mars 3 made the first soft landing — but transmitted for only twenty seconds.

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:29.213
Viking 1 and Viking 2, in 1976, took the first photographs from the surface.

00:00:29.213 --> 00:00:32.295
Click Pathfinder's marker, in Ares Vallis.

00:00:32.295 --> 00:00:34.204
Pathfinder landed in 1997.

00:00:34.204 --> 00:00:42.204
It carried Sojourner — about the size of a microwave oven — the first wheels to ever drive on another planet.

00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:45.727
Sojourner drove about a hundred meters in total.

00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:48.957
Then the lander died, and Sojourner with it.

00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:51.306
Click Curiosity, in Gale Crater.

00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:53.141
Curiosity landed in 2012.

00:00:53.141 --> 00:00:55.343
It is the size of a small car.

00:00:55.343 --> 00:01:06.499
It has driven over thirty kilometers across the floor of Gale Crater and up the lower slopes of Mount Sharp — and as of this moment, it is still moving.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:09.215
Click Perseverance, in Jezero Crater.

00:01:09.215 --> 00:01:16.482
Perseverance is Curiosity's cousin — same chassis design, different instruments, different mission.

00:01:16.482 --> 00:01:21.766
It carries the first helicopter ever flown on another planet, Ingenuity.

00:01:21.766 --> 00:01:27.565
Ingenuity made seventy-two flights before it broke a rotor blade in early 2024.

00:01:27.565 --> 00:01:29.987
Look at the orbiters around Mars.

00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:32.042
Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter.

00:01:32.042 --> 00:01:32.996
Mars Express.

00:01:32.996 --> 00:01:33.437
MAVEN.

00:01:33.437 --> 00:01:34.391
Mars Odyssey.

00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:36.299
ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter.

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:37.914
Mangalyaan from India.

00:01:37.914 --> 00:01:40.483
Hope from the United Arab Emirates.

00:01:40.483 --> 00:01:42.024
Tianwen-1 from China.

00:01:42.024 --> 00:01:46.502
Eleven orbiters work, or recently worked, in the Martian sky.

00:01:46.502 --> 00:01:54.135
They photograph the surface, sniff the atmosphere, relay signals from the rovers, watch the dust storms.

00:01:54.135 --> 00:02:01.989
Click any surface marker and the right panel opens with the mission's history, science return, and gallery.

00:02:01.989 --> 00:02:07.420
Where a rover has driven, the traverse is drawn on the map as a thin line.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:17.109
Zoom in to Curiosity in Gale Crater to see its full route since 2012 — every sol's drive recorded as a faint trace across the plain.

00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:21.440
Twenty surface missions have arrived here over sixty years.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.963
Most of them are still here — silent, but there.

00:02:24.963 --> 00:02:26.137
Look at the map.

00:02:26.137 --> 00:02:27.238
Read the dates.

00:02:27.238 --> 00:02:32.376
Notice how Mars has slowly stopped being a place humans only fly past.

